Read this if you read nothing else:
---> Its a bad idea to spin beyond your timeslice.
     <You want to park if you switch out spinning>

---> Its a bad idea to spin when lock owner is not running.
     <You want to cheaply find this out and abort spinning>

The rest discusses how to accomplish the above two
----------------------------------------------
As you have found out there are two problems with the adaptive / spinning part:

<a> If the lock owner gets kicked out then all/any spinning operation is a waste of time.

    Furthermore threads spinning for their entire timeslice could possibly be blocking thread
    owner from getting onto the CPU to do its lock release. Effects system fwd progress

<b> The idea behind spinning/adaptive being fast lock handover such that extra context switches
    can be avoided. DaveD summarizes that very well below 
    	http://blogs.oracle.com/dave/entry/inverted_schedctl_usage_in_the

    If you do get switched out while spinning next time around you do not want to spin again but
    rather park/block.


